From: lubkin@apollo.hp.com
Subject: Colour linguistics

>From my days in linguistics, I recall that no two words are identical.  You
can imagine that each word in any language fills a region in n-space.  If two
words are close by, we consider them equivalent, but they will not be 
identical.

I read a book on semantics once that illustrated this fact by looking at
color words in different languages.  All languages have words that together
cover the full visual spectrum, but they break it up differently.  Hence you
see languages with far fewer color words than English, or just as many, but
covering different spectral ranges.

And two words that seem to be identical, like "rouge" and "red", turn out not 
to be.  There have been studies where subjects were shown colors and asked to 
name them, and the "rouge" set was not identical to the "red" set.  (I'm not 
sure about rouge/red per se; it's just meant as an example.)

    
-- David Lubkin.

