From: altar@beaufort.sfu.ca (Ted Wayn Altar)
Subject: Health Advantages of Vegetarianism
Organization: Simon Fraser University, Burnaby, B.C., Canada
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 1992 15:52:43 GMT

I guess here on this conference we all know that vegetarianism
incurs some pretty significant health advantages.  But how much
of an advantage might there be?  An estimate of the effect size
is useful so as to compare, say, the relative advantages of other
possible dietary regimes.  To date, the evidence would indicate
that no omnivorous diet incurs the same advantages as does a
vegetarian diet.  To me, this would suggest that the less meat
you eat, the better and that none is probably best.  The same,
however, would seem to also apply to diary/egg consumption.  For
instance, comparing just the non-smoking Seventh Day Adventists
men we Phillips et al. (1978) found that:

=============================================================
|   OBSERVED-TO-EXPECTED CORONARY HEART DISEASE MORTALITY   |
|                     ADVENTIST MEN                         |
|                                                           |
|  Total Vegetarians                14%                     |
|  Lacto-Ovo-Vegetarians            39%                     |
|  Meat Users                       56%                     |
|                                                           |
=============================================================
Note that even the SDA meat users have a lower than expected
mortality which can be accounted for in large part by the fact
that even meat eating SDA's eat less meat then North Americans
in general.

To return to our Seventh Day Adventists again, consider the
following from Phillips R. et al. "Environmental Aspects of
Cancer: The Role of Macro and Micro Components of Foods" 1983.

=================================================================
|           Meat Use and Ovary Cancer Death Rates:              |
|                                                               |
|  Lacto-ovo-vegetarian Adventists                15.9/100,000  |
|  Adventists using meat 1-3 times per week       18.0/100,000  |
|  General population                             24.0/100,000  |
|                                                               |
=================================================================

To help make this point, permit me to re-post some more tables
I've already posted here some months earlier:

+++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
+     RELATIVE PERCENTAGE OF LIFE STYLE FACTORS CONTRIBUTING    +
+ TO A DECREASED LIFE EXPECTANCY FROM ATHEROSCLEROSIS & CANCER  +
+                                                               +
+           Tobacco                                 25%         +
+           Meat                                    25%         +
+           diary Products                          15%         +
+           eggs, high fat                                      +
+           foods                                               +
+           Obesity                                 10%         +
+           Lack of Exercise                        10%         +
+           Stress, refined sugars,                 15%         +
+           other factors                                       +
+++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

 The above chart is useful in providing one estimate of the
relative contribution of life style factors for an increased risk
for atherosclerosis and cancer.  This result was first presented
at an annual meeting of the American Association for the
Advancement of Science by John Scharffenberg (M.D., M.P.H.) who
is Adjunct Professor of Nutrition at Loma Linda University and is
the Vice President of the Pacific Health Education Center (also
published in Scharffenberg's "Problems with Meat", 1989). These
estimates of the relative contribution of life style factors are
based upon epidemological data.

In particular, Scharffenberg employed epidemiological data educed
from some vegetarian populations (e.g., Seventh Day Adventists).
It turns out from Scharffenberg's data that the best diet for
human beings in terms of health and longevity is vegetarian.  In
general, vegetarians live about 6 years longer than their meat
eating counterparts (i.e., meat-eating Seventh Day Adventists).
If we select as our comparison group only those Seventh Day
adventists who still eat meat and who do not smoke, it turns out
that their total vegetarian counterparts still out live them by 3
years.  hence, even when we use a somewhat conservative
comparison group (remember, even though not all sda's are
vegetarian but on the whole they would still eat less meat then
the average north american) and thereby control for known life
style hazards like smoking, vegetarians still live longer (about
3 years) than even moderate meat eaters, and here we are talking
a compable comparison group of meat eaters who otherwise share
essentially the same life style.  This is still a substantial
difference and it would appear that the hazards of meat eating
are on the same level as that of smoking (which as we have seen
shortens this comparison group's lifespan by about 3 years).

Also, keep in mind that the observed-to-expected coronary heart
disease mortality among total vegetarians is only 14%.
Vegetarians, of course, do have heart attacks but they occur
approximately 20 years later in life than for meat eaters.  Thus,
not only is longevity increased but so is the quality of health
for the life span that one does live out.  This is important,
especially if we hope to live a robust and vital life and not end
up being a valetudinarian or invalid during our "golden" years,
which indeed should be golden.

    Author:    Snowdon DA.  Phillips RL.  
