Date: Wed, 16 Jun 1993 12:17:45 -0700 (PDT)
From: Scott C DeLancey <delancey@darkwing.uoregon.edu>
Subject: Colour linguistics

On Wed, 16 Jun 1993 lubkin@apollo.hp.com wrote:

> I read a book on semantics once that illustrated this fact by looking at
> color words in different languages.  All languages have words that together
> cover the full visual spectrum, but they break it up differently.  Hence you
> see languages with far fewer color words than English, or just as many, but
> covering different spectral ranges.

I was taught this once, too, but it turns out to be a bit of an exaggeration.
Languages agree very closely on the focal value for color terms--i.e. an
English speaker and a Chinese speaker may disagree on what shades they
would be willing to call marginal examples of 'red', but they will agree
on what shade is the best example of red.  So, if a language has only
four color terms (in which case they will be 'white', 'black', 'red', and
'grue'), its speakers will probably apply their equivalent of 'red' to
shades that English speakers will insist on calling something else--but
anything that is 'red' in English will be 'red' in that language also.
     You DON'T find--and this is a significant discovery--slippage
across languages such that one might have a word which applies to half
the English 'red' range, all of 'orange', and some 'yellow', but excludes  
the other half of English 'red'.  Color terms in all languages are 
organized around the same focal values; languages vary in how finely
they divide up the spectrum, but not in the central values for basic
color terms.   (Refs. available if anybody wants them).

