Date: Wed, 16 Jun 93 13:20:47 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: SOC: Are 70% of Extropians Firstborns? 

A total of 32 extropians have responded to my poll.  Only 9 of these
folk were not firstborns!  The average number of children was 2.8 and
only 6 were only children.

I found this surprising.  Tim May claims that "the majority of
scientists are first-born", and I'd be interested to hear the source
of this datum.

I realize that there is some ambiguity in "firstborn", but I was just
trying to do a quick and dirty poll, and so took the simplest
definition I could think of, restricting to just children of the same
two parents.  Others are welcome to do a more careful poll.

I've decide to not conclude much from the answer to my third question,
about whether people thought they were more likely to "support" new
theories, as there seems to be too much variation in what this can
mean.  Again, someone else might do a more careful poll.

Here is the processed data, a set (order n-children):

((1 3)(4 4)(2 4)(1 2)(4 5)(3 3)(1 3)(1 2)(1 3)(1 3)(1 1)(1 7)(1 1)(2 2)
(1 1)(1 2)(2 3)(1 5)(1 3)(1 1)(1 3)(1 2)(1 2)(1 3)(1 4)(1 1)(1 2)(1 1)
(1 4)(1 2)(1 5)(1 2))

I could send all the messages to someone who wants to check Tim's
claim:

>And the heated arguments on this list show that many of us, at least
>the active posters (who I would bet are 80% first borns!)

Anyway, either we are some special set of firstborns, or we are in
fact less likely to support a scientific revolution than others.  Hard
to say which.  

Robin Hanson

