My son is not circumcised. My sister's son is. 

We decided not to have Lyle circumcised after reading the literature and 
finding that there are only one or two studies that show any significant
difference in disease between circumcised and uncircumcised males. The
"disease" we are talking about, by the way is primarily yeast infections,
and I believe a slightly higher rate of NSUTI (non-specific urinary tract
infection). 

The rest of the huge number of studies found no statistical difference.

My sister made the decision by default. She didn't think about it, and 
when they asked, her husband said "Sure". (He is circumcised). 

After seeing the obvious pain that her son was in for the first week
of his life, and the rather nasty looking wound, I was very glad that 
we decided the way we did.

Remember that at that age, the foreskin has not separated from the glans,
so circumcision requires surgically separating the two and removing the 
former. It is done with no anaesthesia.

My sister was very upset afterward. In her words, the baby that she got 
back after the procedure was not the happy, smiling baby that went in,
and didn't return to being so for about a week.

